    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The spatial distribution of faults formed in porous sandstone from shearing of joint zones are characterized by measurements along kilometer scale scanlines in the Valley of Fire State Park, Nevada. Results show that fractures are clustered near faults and form a dense fracture network in the fault zone periphery. There is a systematic widening of the fracture networks with increasing fault slip though the most extensively fractured zones occur near fault intersections or in the interval between closely spaced faults. Fracture and fault spacing data have both lognormal and fractal distributions. The scanline data are compared to core fracture and fault data from the Claire Field, North Sea. The subsurface data show clustering of fractures near larger shear zones and similarities to the surface data in (1) the angular relationship between faults and nearby fractures, and (2) fractal dimensions for fault spacing distributions. These data suggest that the fault zones in the Valley of Fire and the Clair Field formed by the same process, and therefore have similar fluid flow properties and sub-seismic fault distributions.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
